Ah I think I get what you mean. You say that the method should just return the difference in frames rather than seconds? That's actually was my initial idea but I thought that it's not as user-friendly as returning the time in seconds, not sure about that.

> It does not look like so. See the Action struct in input_default.h

That's was my first impression too, until I stumbled upon this:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/dace1ec9126c752792a0566cfd9485a8ad40c0d1/main/input_default.cpp#L425-L440

That's the only part which seems to populate input action states on event parsing. Notice this line of code:
```cpp
is_action_pressed(E->key()) != p_event->is_action_pressed(E->key())
```
It means that the action's state is only updated on change.

So the `Action` struct should actually be more explicit regarding timestamp names (notice `last_*` prefix):
```cpp
struct Action {
	uint64_t last_physics_frame;
	uint64_t last_idle_frame;
	bool pressed;
	float strength;
};
```
And by the way, the `action_state` map is essentially empty if no actions been pressed during the game, so `Input.get_action_duration()` would not actually report the "unpressed" time but `0.0`. I wonder whether it makes sense to "pre-populate" the `action_state` from the `InputMap` on `Input` initialization to help this.

I suppose you're talking about using `OS.get_ticks_usec` for timestamps then. But what if a game lags so much that the tick difference gets too big for a single frame?

Here are the changes to the method:
```cpp
float InputDefault::get_action_duration(const StringName &p_action) const {
	const Map<StringName, Action>::Element *E = action_state.find(p_action);
	if (!E)
		return 0;
	return (OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec() - E->get().timestamp) / 1000000.0f;
}
```

`is_equal_approx(Input.get_action_duration("jump"), 0.0)` never reports `True` with this, unlike with the current change.

So I suppose the current changes are more robust even with possible issues with fluctuating FPS in my opinion.

