I feel hesitant to add logic to `Path3D`. The reason is that, currently its only responsibility is an empty shell that hold the resource `Curve3D` into the scene tree (`debug_instance` and `debug_mesh` notwithstanding).

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/13f1d8096032bf572d5071c5bf9f61de0a055fed/scene/3d/path_3d.h#L37-L45

Adding the `artistic control` logic on `Path3D` will destroy this simplicity and create insymmetry with the 2D counterpart, creating problems in future.

> get the transform with the corrected posture, which is the wanted behavior by @Zireael07

From the previous posts, I get the feeling that @Zireael07 have a very similar use case as my own (If I am wrong, feel free correct me). We mainly need the `rotation frame`(posture) from the curve, and need no or very simple `artistic control`. Then we can just do the necessary rotation correction in our own code. It's quite simple.

For example, to lock rotation to the up vector
```c++
// In c++, can be directly translated into GDScript
Vector3 euler = frame.get_euler_normalized(EulerOrder::YXZ);
euler[0] = 0;
euler[2] = 0;
frame = Basis::from_euler(euler, EulerOrder::YXZ);
```


So I suggest we keep the `artistic control` on `PathFollow3D`, which make the life much easier for people who want to implement more sophisticated `artistic control` algorithms like constraints and drivers.

On the other hand, if @Zireael07 do need full blown `artistic control` on `Path3D` or If you insist we must do `artistic control` on `Path3D`, I think I can live with it.

