`static` is used for something which is global/reusable in the class, as a cache system. So if you always re-assign it a new string, there's no point in making it static. It's only useful in this kind of construct:
```cpp
static String some_string;
if (some_string.is_empty()) { // True the first time, won't be true next time so we skip the calculation.
    some_string = some_complex_method();
}
return some_string;
```

Here `static` can make sense to avoid opening OS files repeatedly for information that won't change, but it's a bit trickier as you also have a fallback system for non-systemd systems. So the whole logic should be something like this:

```cpp
static String distribution_name;
if (!distribution_name.is_empty()) {
	return distribution_name;
}

// Check first if systemd has the info.
distribution_name = get_systemd_os_release_info_value("NAME");
if (!distribution_name.is_empty()) {
	return distribution_name;
}

// Otherwise utsname can provide a decent value for BSD family.
struct utsname uts; 
uname(&uts);
distribution_name = uts.sysname;
if (!distribution_name.is_empty()) {
	return distribution_name;
}

// Just in case all failed, assign a fallback.
distribution_name = "<unknown>";

return distribution_name;
```

Same for `get_version`.

